1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,062
You get up for a glass of water. That's it, nothing dramatic.

2
00:00:04,062 --> 00:00:08,875
But before you've made it halfway to the kitchen there's a soft thump somewhere behind you,

3
00:00:08,875 --> 00:00:12,127
and you already know what it is. The cat's up too.

4
00:00:12,127 --> 00:00:15,361
You head to the bathroom and a paw slides under the door,

5
00:00:15,361 --> 00:00:19,641
feeling around like it's lost something. You move from the couch to the desk,

6
00:00:19,641 --> 00:00:24,663
and ten seconds later, guess who's moved from the couch to the desk. It isn't hungry.

7
00:00:24,663 --> 00:00:28,683
It doesn't want to play. It just wants to be wherever you happen to be.

8
00:00:28,683 --> 00:00:33,385
Which is a bit odd, honestly. Cats are meant to be the independent ones.

9
00:00:33,385 --> 00:00:36,359
Dogs follow you around, that's their whole deal.

10
00:00:36,359 --> 00:00:41,829
Cats, or so the story goes, barely register that you exist until the bowl runs empty.

11
00:00:41,829 --> 00:00:46,237
So why does an animal famous for not caring trail after you like a small,

12
00:00:46,237 --> 00:00:51,664
furry shadow with opinions? The answer, as far as anyone can tell, is a mix of things.

13
00:00:51,664 --> 00:00:58,457
Some ancient history. Some baby behavior that never quite switched off. A surprising amount of brainpower.

14
00:00:58,457 --> 00:01:03,159
And a handful of very practical, very cat reasons. Let's get into it.

15
00:01:03,159 --> 00:01:07,285
Start with the reputation, because that's where the confusion comes from.

16
00:01:07,285 --> 00:01:11,880
The idea of the aloof, solitary cat really comes from the way cats hunt.

17
00:01:11,880 --> 00:01:16,390
A cat hunts alone. It stalks, it waits, it pounces, and it does not share.

18
00:01:16,390 --> 00:01:22,372
Lions are the exception, but nearly every other wild cat species lives and hunts on its own.

19
00:01:22,372 --> 00:01:26,161
So people looked at the little one on the sofa and assumed, fair enough,

20
00:01:26,161 --> 00:01:29,071
it must be a loner too. Here's the thing though.

21
00:01:29,071 --> 00:01:32,173
Hunting alone and living alone aren't the same.

22
00:01:32,173 --> 00:01:35,659
And the story of how cats ended up in our houses suggests

23
00:01:35,659 --> 00:01:39,273
they were never quite as solitary as we gave them credit for.

24
00:01:39,273 --> 00:01:45,255
Roughly ten thousand years ago, people in the Near East started farming. Farming meant storing grain.

25
00:01:45,255 --> 00:01:48,527
Stored grain meant mice, and I mean a lot of mice.

26
00:01:48,527 --> 00:01:51,677
And the mice, naturally, brought in the local wildcats,

27
00:01:51,677 --> 00:01:56,827
who figured out pretty quickly that human villages were the best hunting grounds for miles.

28
00:01:56,827 --> 00:02:01,422
Nobody actually tamed those cats. They more or less tamed themselves.

29
00:02:01,422 --> 00:02:05,766
The bolder, calmer ones, the ones that could put up with people, got the most food,

30
00:02:05,766 --> 00:02:11,044
raised the most kittens, and over a long, long time turned into a new kind of animal.

31
00:02:11,044 --> 00:02:15,447
By about nine and a half thousand years ago, things were already pretty close.

32
00:02:15,447 --> 00:02:20,661
On the island of Cyprus, archaeologists dug up a cat buried right beside a human.

33
00:02:20,661 --> 00:02:25,513
Now, cats aren't native to Cyprus. Somebody brought that cat over by boat.

34
00:02:25,513 --> 00:02:28,572
And somebody cared enough to bury it with a person.

35
00:02:28,572 --> 00:02:31,397
So we go back a long way, us and cats.

36
00:02:31,397 --> 00:02:36,013
And when cats have enough to eat, it turns out they don't stay solitary at all.

37
00:02:36,013 --> 00:02:41,249
Look at a feral colony, the groups you see hanging around farms and docks and back alleys.

38
00:02:41,249 --> 00:02:47,380
At the heart of most of them there's a cluster of related females. Mothers, daughters, sisters, aunts.

39
00:02:47,380 --> 00:02:51,335
They groom each other. They sleep piled together in one warm heap.

40
00:02:51,335 --> 00:02:56,336
They rub against each other when they meet, and they'll even help raise each other's kittens.

41
00:02:56,336 --> 00:03:00,910
The lone hunter, once it clocks off, is actually a fairly social creature.

42
00:03:00,910 --> 00:03:04,780
Which brings us to the biggest reason your cat follows you around.

43
00:03:04,780 --> 00:03:09,759
From where it's standing, you're not really an owner. You're something much closer to a parent.

44
00:03:09,759 --> 00:03:13,869
Kittens have this short window, somewhere between two and seven weeks old,

45
00:03:13,869 --> 00:03:17,035
when they work out who's safe and who counts as family.

46
00:03:17,035 --> 00:03:19,752
A kitten that gets handled by people during

47
00:03:19,752 --> 00:03:23,686
that stretch grows up treating humans as part of its social world.

48
00:03:23,686 --> 00:03:27,982
And an awful lot of what your adult cat does every day is kitten behavior

49
00:03:27,982 --> 00:03:33,395
that simply never got turned off. Take kneading. That rhythmic pushing with the front paws?

50
00:03:33,395 --> 00:03:37,607
That's what kittens do against their mother's belly to get the milk flowing.

51
00:03:37,607 --> 00:03:40,645
Your cat does it on your lap. Or take meowing.

52
00:03:40,645 --> 00:03:46,392
Adult cats hardly ever meow at each other. They talk with scent, with body language, with touch.

53
00:03:46,392 --> 00:03:49,729
Kittens meow at their mother when they're cold or hungry.

54
00:03:49,729 --> 00:03:55,241
Grown cats keep meowing at exactly one species, and it's us. Then there's the tail.

55
00:03:55,241 --> 00:03:58,220
When your cat trots toward you with its tail straight up,

56
00:03:58,220 --> 00:04:01,557
maybe with a little hook at the very tip, that's a greeting.

57
00:04:01,557 --> 00:04:05,299
Kittens hold their tails like that when they walk up to their mother.

58
00:04:05,299 --> 00:04:12,241
In cat colonies, researchers found that the tail-up signal usually goes from lower-ranking cats to higher-ranking ones.

59
00:04:12,241 --> 00:04:15,983
So it's a friendly hello, but with a little bit of respect baked in.

60
00:04:15,983 --> 00:04:19,751
When your cat follows you down the hallway, tail high like a flag,

61
00:04:19,751 --> 00:04:22,938
it's treating you like the senior member of the family.

62
00:04:22,938 --> 00:04:26,872
For a long time, though, nobody took any of this very seriously.

63
00:04:26,872 --> 00:04:31,765
Dogs got all the attachment studies. Cats were filed under "in it for the food."

64
00:04:31,765 --> 00:04:36,595
Then, in twenty nineteen, a team of researchers in the United States decided

65
00:04:36,595 --> 00:04:40,870
to test cats the same way scientists test human babies, and dogs.

66
00:04:40,870 --> 00:04:44,399
The test is called the secure base test, and it's simple.

67
00:04:44,399 --> 00:04:48,695
A cat and its owner go into an unfamiliar room together for two minutes.

68
00:04:48,695 --> 00:04:53,888
Then the owner leaves, and the cat sits alone for two minutes. Then the owner comes back.

69
00:04:53,888 --> 00:04:59,597
The bit that matters is the reunion. A securely attached cat greets its person, calms down,

70
00:04:59,597 --> 00:05:02,299
and then wanders off to explore the room again,

71
00:05:02,299 --> 00:05:08,259
using that person as a sort of safe home base. An insecurely attached cat does something else.

72
00:05:08,259 --> 00:05:13,580
It clings and can't settle, or it ignores the owner completely, or it bolts and hides.

73
00:05:13,580 --> 00:05:18,858
About sixty-four percent of the kittens in that study were securely attached to their owners.

74
00:05:18,858 --> 00:05:24,243
That's almost exactly the same as human babies, where it's around sixty-five percent.

75
00:05:24,243 --> 00:05:28,483
And it's very close to what's been found in dogs. So the aloof cat?

76
00:05:28,483 --> 00:05:33,718
Mostly a myth, it seems. A study from twenty seventeen pushed it even further.

77
00:05:33,718 --> 00:05:37,439
Researchers gave cats a choice between four kinds of things.

78
00:05:37,439 --> 00:05:41,586
Food, toys, interesting smells, and a person who'd interact with them.

79
00:05:41,586 --> 00:05:45,392
Each cat had already gone a few hours without food beforehand.

80
00:05:45,392 --> 00:05:51,395
And still, half the cats picked the human over everything else. Food included. Food came second.

81
00:05:51,395 --> 00:05:54,668
So a big part of the answer really is that simple.

82
00:05:54,668 --> 00:05:59,199
Plenty of cats follow you because they like you, and being near you feels safe.

83
00:05:59,199 --> 00:06:03,304
But they're also keeping tabs on you. And they're weirdly good at it.

84
00:06:03,304 --> 00:06:08,689
In twenty nineteen, researchers in Japan showed that cats can recognize their own names.

85
00:06:08,689 --> 00:06:13,651
Even with the name tucked into a list of similar-sounding words, the cats picked it out,

86
00:06:13,651 --> 00:06:20,401
moving their ears, turning their heads. Then in twenty twenty-one another Japanese team tried something stranger.

87
00:06:20,401 --> 00:06:24,639
They played a cat its owner's voice from one side of a room, and then,

88
00:06:24,639 --> 00:06:28,210
a moment later, from a speaker in a totally different spot.

89
00:06:28,210 --> 00:06:32,194
The cats looked surprised. Like, wait, how did you get over there?

90
00:06:32,194 --> 00:06:35,935
Which suggests a cat carries around a kind of mental map of

91
00:06:35,935 --> 00:06:39,059
where you are in the house, even when it can't see you.

92
00:06:39,059 --> 00:06:42,331
Following you is how it keeps that map up to date.

93
00:06:42,331 --> 00:06:45,775
When you vanish into another room, you've dropped off the map.

94
00:06:45,775 --> 00:06:50,007
And the cat wants to know where you went. And that leads to territory.

95
00:06:50,007 --> 00:06:55,072
Cats are deeply territorial, and your home is their territory. You're part of it.

96
00:06:55,072 --> 00:06:57,987
A cat spends a good chunk of its day patrolling,

97
00:06:57,987 --> 00:07:00,811
checking that everything's where it ought to be.

98
00:07:00,811 --> 00:07:03,214
So when you get up and walk into a room,

99
00:07:03,214 --> 00:07:07,617
the cat comes along to see what's going on in that corner of its little kingdom.

100
00:07:07,617 --> 00:07:10,698
It's also why cats can't stand closed doors.

101
00:07:10,698 --> 00:07:15,123
A closed door means a piece of the territory has suddenly gone out of reach.

102
00:07:15,123 --> 00:07:20,017
The cat doesn't necessarily want to be in that room. It wants to know it could be.

103
00:07:20,017 --> 00:07:24,766
Which is why, the second you open the door, it'll often just peek in, look satisfied,

104
00:07:24,766 --> 00:07:28,871
and stroll off again. Cats mark their territory with scent, too.

105
00:07:28,871 --> 00:07:32,719
They've got glands on their cheeks, their chin, and the base of the tail.

106
00:07:32,719 --> 00:07:36,381
So when your cat rubs its face along your legs while it follows you,

107
00:07:36,381 --> 00:07:39,786
it's leaving its scent on you. Mixing its smell with yours,

108
00:07:39,786 --> 00:07:44,680
the same way colony cats rub against each other to build one shared group smell.

109
00:07:44,680 --> 00:07:51,494
In other words, you're being labeled. Now for the less romantic reasons. Cats are extremely practical animals.

110
00:07:51,494 --> 00:07:54,511
First, food. You're the one who opens the cans.

111
00:07:54,511 --> 00:07:57,377
A cat doesn't need to be a genius to notice

112
00:07:57,377 --> 00:08:00,799
that good things tend to happen when you walk into the kitchen.

113
00:08:00,799 --> 00:08:06,440
So it tags along, just in case. Lots of cats learn exactly which footsteps mean dinner.

114
00:08:06,440 --> 00:08:10,907
Second, routine. Cats adore routine. They learn the whole shape of your day.

115
00:08:10,907 --> 00:08:15,033
When you wake up, when you eat, when you finally sit down in the evening.

116
00:08:15,033 --> 00:08:18,285
Following you is partly a way of staying in sync.

117
00:08:18,285 --> 00:08:20,959
Ever noticed how a cat can be dead asleep

118
00:08:20,959 --> 00:08:24,581
and still turn up the moment you start your bedtime routine?

119
00:08:24,581 --> 00:08:28,451
It's been paying attention the whole time. Third, warmth.

120
00:08:28,451 --> 00:08:33,093
A cat is comfortable at somewhere around thirty to thirty-eight degrees Celsius,

121
00:08:33,093 --> 00:08:36,387
which is a lot warmer than most of us keep our homes.

122
00:08:36,387 --> 00:08:40,364
And you're a warm body. Your lap is warm. Your laptop is warm.

123
00:08:40,364 --> 00:08:43,423
The spot on the bed you just got out of is warm.

124
00:08:43,423 --> 00:08:48,040
The cat follows the heat, and very often the heat is you. Fourth, boredom.

125
00:08:48,040 --> 00:08:52,000
Most house cats live indoors these days. That keeps them safe,

126
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:56,979
sure, but it also means the most interesting thing in their entire world is often you.

127
00:08:56,979 --> 00:09:00,444
You move stuff. You make noises. You open doors.

128
00:09:00,444 --> 00:09:06,767
For an indoor cat, following you around is basically the evening's entertainment. And then, the bathroom.

129
00:09:06,767 --> 00:09:10,407
Almost every cat owner has had the cat sit there and stare at them,

130
00:09:10,407 --> 00:09:14,064
or yell outside the door like something terrible is happening.

131
00:09:14,064 --> 00:09:16,782
From the cat's side, it kind of makes sense.

132
00:09:16,782 --> 00:09:21,335
The bathroom is a small room with a door, and closed doors are suspicious.

133
00:09:21,335 --> 00:09:26,079
Once you're in there, you're sitting still, at roughly cat eye level, with nowhere to go.

134
00:09:26,079 --> 00:09:31,848
A captive audience, pretty much. Some cats also just love the tap, the sink, the cool tiles.

135
00:09:31,848 --> 00:09:37,237
And there's one more theory floating around. Cats are vulnerable when they use the litter box,

136
00:09:37,237 --> 00:09:42,281
so some people think your cat might be standing guard while you're in the same, well, position.

137
00:09:42,281 --> 00:09:45,788
Nobody's proven that one. It's a nice idea, though.

138
00:09:45,788 --> 00:09:49,871
Not every cat follows people around to the same degree, of course.

139
00:09:49,871 --> 00:09:52,824
Some breeds are known for being properly clingy.

140
00:09:52,824 --> 00:09:59,041
Siamese, Burmese, Abyssinians, Ragdolls and Sphynx cats all have a reputation as velcro cats.

141
00:09:59,041 --> 00:10:03,619
A cat's own history matters too. Kittens that were bottle-raised by people,

142
00:10:03,619 --> 00:10:08,641
or taken from their mother very early, often grow up extra attached to humans.

143
00:10:08,641 --> 00:10:13,300
And a cat that lives alone with one person usually bonds much more strongly

144
00:10:13,300 --> 00:10:16,893
to that person than a cat in a busy, noisy household.

145
00:10:16,893 --> 00:10:21,275
So, most of the time, a cat that follows you everywhere is a good sign.

146
00:10:21,275 --> 00:10:24,867
There are moments, though, when it's worth paying closer attention.

147
00:10:24,867 --> 00:10:30,273
In twenty twenty, researchers in Brazil surveyed the owners of more than two hundred cats.

148
00:10:30,273 --> 00:10:36,298
About thirteen and a half percent of those cats showed signs of what's called separation-related problems.

149
00:10:36,298 --> 00:10:42,323
These cats didn't just follow their owners around. They got genuinely distressed when the owner left.

150
00:10:42,323 --> 00:10:47,029
The signs included peeing or pooping outside the litter box, destroying things,

151
00:10:47,029 --> 00:10:52,734
crying or yowling for long stretches, and over-grooming until patches of fur came away.

152
00:10:52,734 --> 00:10:57,419
The study also found these problems turned up more in homes with no other pets,

153
00:10:57,419 --> 00:11:00,329
and in homes where the cat had no toys.

154
00:11:00,329 --> 00:11:03,666
The other thing to keep an eye on is sudden change.

155
00:11:03,666 --> 00:11:09,183
If a fairly independent cat abruptly starts following you everywhere, especially an older cat,

156
00:11:09,183 --> 00:11:14,871
that can be a sign something's off. Pain, an overactive thyroid, losing its sight or its hearing,

157
00:11:14,871 --> 00:11:18,725
or a kind of age-related confusion a lot like dementia,

158
00:11:18,725 --> 00:11:23,090
any of those can make a cat suddenly clingy. A cat that feels unwell,

159
00:11:23,090 --> 00:11:28,304
or unsure of its surroundings, will often stick close to the one thing that still feels safe.

160
00:11:28,304 --> 00:11:32,558
If the change comes out of nowhere, a trip to the vet is a good idea.

161
00:11:32,558 --> 00:11:36,265
And if your cat's shadowing is healthy but, let's say, a bit much?

162
00:11:36,265 --> 00:11:40,011
There are easy fixes. Play with it properly, with a wand toy,

163
00:11:40,011 --> 00:11:43,651
for ten or fifteen minutes before you leave the house or head to bed,

164
00:11:43,651 --> 00:11:46,433
so it gets to hunt, catch, and then rest.

165
00:11:46,433 --> 00:11:49,642
Use puzzle feeders, so food takes a bit of work.

166
00:11:49,642 --> 00:11:53,469
Put a perch by a window so there's something to watch other than you.

167
00:11:53,469 --> 00:11:57,894
Keep a steady routine. And please don't punish a cat for following you.

168
00:11:57,894 --> 00:12:01,039
It isn't misbehaving. It's just looking for you.

169
00:12:01,039 --> 00:12:05,212
If you want to give something back, try the slow blink. In twenty twenty,

170
00:12:05,212 --> 00:12:10,985
scientists at the University of Sussex found that when people slowly narrowed their eyes at a cat,

171
00:12:10,985 --> 00:12:14,621
the cat was much more likely to slowly blink right back.

172
00:12:14,621 --> 00:12:19,536
Cats were also more likely to walk up to a stranger who'd slow blinked at them first.

173
00:12:19,536 --> 00:12:24,238
It's about as close as we get to smiling at a cat in its own language. So.

174
00:12:24,238 --> 00:12:28,667
Why do cats follow their owners everywhere? Because for ten thousand years,

175
00:12:28,667 --> 00:12:33,822
they've been choosing to live alongside us. Because to them you're part parent, part family,

176
00:12:33,822 --> 00:12:39,527
and part territory. Because they like you more than most people assume, often more than food.

177
00:12:39,527 --> 00:12:45,065
Because they want to know where you are. And sometimes, honestly, because you're warm, you're interesting,

178
00:12:45,065 --> 00:12:49,063
and you might just be heading to the kitchen. So next time you get up for

179
00:12:49,063 --> 00:12:52,169
that glass of water and hear the soft thump behind you,

180
00:12:52,169 --> 00:12:55,527
you'll know what it means. It's not just a nosy cat.

181
00:12:55,527 --> 00:13:00,400
It's a small animal that's decided, for its own reasons, that you're worth following.
